body='''# Independent ReLU revision 1 source review

Recommendation: suitable for finite-source integration with eight explicitly registered scalar conformance families deferred. No structural blocker was found for the frozen receipt below. Root must supply its own substantive review and exact pins, integrate the authority, and run current repository gates. This peer review makes no acceptance increment.

Producer receipt: `e219fb011932538d411b02b85a5e624e7d617198faca6730a953ddac2db5e46e`.
Public declaration: `VeriTile.Bench.TritonBenchG.ReluTritonKernel.relu_actual_source_outcomes` in `ActualCorrect.lean`.

## Source correspondence and complete scope

I read the original Python, all eight proposed modules, the public statement, runtime source, authority proposal and adapter. The original kernel has seven statements. `original_ast_lowering` checks all seven against the existing source AST. Finite i32 pid multiplication and addition, all four constexpr comparison-promotion branches, signed extension of each pointer offset and u64 address wrap are retained. There is no substituted N<=2^31 premise. Nonrepresentable N fails frontend processing; larger representable N can compile while failing native signed-int grid parsing. Actual launched pids are bounded independently of N. The independent 264-profile matrix checks all 22 dtypes at twelve boundaries, including failure precedence when N=2^64.

The source interpreter retains the load and comparison/select before the pid-zero branch. Registers are mathematical functions, but applicability is checked separately for every active lane of every launched pid. `ProgramSafe` requires all pre-gate reads even for a pid with no writes, and `GridSafe` quantifies over the entire launch. An independent Lean regression demonstrates that denying pid1/lane0's read rejects safety for N=1025 although that program has no output writes. Masked inactive words may be arbitrary and do not affect returned bytes.

All 22 canonical dtype routes are represented: thirteen scalar routes, five missing signature mappings, two unsupported fnuz signatures and two unsupported ordered fp8 comparisons. Integer signed/unsigned comparisons and normalization of all 256 bool storage bytes are concrete. Signed/unsigned conversion to fp32, fp16 comparison and extension, bf16 extension and fp32 comparison, fp32 comparison, and fp64 ordered comparison/narrowing are registered exactly where source output depends on them. Float64 comparison is a concrete finite IEEE word operation whose native correspondence remains an explicit scalar conformance obligation. Literal positive zero is an all-zero finite encoding, not an arbitrary literal callback. Destination float conversions occur in the gated store; select occurs before the gate.

The scope has 404 observation cases over 176 dtype-by-size partitions. Eight adjacent size intervals exhaust Nat, including empty, first/multiple tiles, unsigned i32, launchable/nonlaunchable signed i64, unsigned i64, and nonrepresentable constexpr. The launchable signed-i64 interval ends at N=(2^31-1)*1024 inclusive; the next integer crosses the signed grid parser. All 52 nonempty defined-result cases carry their exact numerical ports; empty, native failure and outside-memory-model observations carry no spurious numerical authority. There are 68 port instances across eight families. Every required port omission was independently rejected.

## Actual pointers, byte accesses and returned memory

`PhysicalView` distinguishes canonical backing base, native device base, actual extracted argument pointer and capacity. `AccessContext` provides one shared native-to-backing map plus per-byte read/write permissions indexed by the current launch device. Tensor owner-device metadata is retained and does not grant or deny access. This permits accessible peer/pinned memory without an owner=current-device assumption. The pinned native `extractPointer` uses CUDA DEVICE_POINTER and can replace raw data_ptr; it bypasses the attribute call for null pointers. `PointerBinding` retains the raw pointer metadata, preserves null extraction, and binds successful nonempty argument pointers and bytewise backing translation. The mapping and ordinary primitive contracts contain no loaded values or final-memory results.

Every active load is checked for native alignment, finite/inbounds native span, permissions for each byte, canonical backing extent and within-word affine translation. Every actual store has corresponding width-four checks. `translated_load` and `translated_store_byte` connect these native accesses to backing bytes. An independent Lean example reads native address 8 through a nonidentity map to canonical 108 on current device 7, with explicit byte permissions. This validates the formal plumbing without claiming a live CUDA access test.

The independent result uses the last covering store found in the reverse write list; bytes untouched by a store come directly from the allocated initial snapshot. `gridFormula` samples initial input bytes independently of execution, and freshness/frame proofs justify that sample through any program order. Complete launch schedules are permutations of all pids, with the same full read applicability. Native pid bounds prove that only pid0 writes. The public result consequently retains arbitrary empty_like contents beyond the first tile rather than claiming a complete tensor ReLU. Input and exterior bytes retain the original memory. Source accesses outside the stated memory model produce an observation carrying the allocated snapshot; this is explicitly not a native exception, rollback, successful return, or final-memory claim.

## Host and native setup

The host retains x.device evaluation, empty_like(float32,x.device), returned-output numel, the unused num_blocks expression, separate grid evaluation, launch and returned metadata. Actual shape, strides, offsets, raw data pointers, backing identity and extent remain fields, including empty and noncontiguous cases. The ordinary allocator contract fixes shape/dtype/device and freshness/frame but leaves initial output bytes arbitrary. Native current-device acquisition precedes dtype binding; compilation precedes native context/grid parsing, then argument extraction and enqueue preparation. Native operations remain fallible and preserve their actual failure state, with no rollback premise. The successful grid parser port carries only a metadata bound, never a kernel value.

The older bounded FiniteCorrect and dependency sources remain unchanged. Source inventory test-harness exclusions retain the previously modeled initializeModule order and first-error behavior; they do not create missing kernel stages or implicit numerical approval.

## Independent validation and limits

- Rebuilt all eight exact candidate modules in a fresh isolated /tmp overlay: 36 clean-axiom checks and six noncircular checks passed. Every rebuilt object hash exactly equals its producer object. All eight current producer source/log/record/object sets were checked. All 578 frozen candidate artifacts remain unchanged.
- Recursively snapshotted 182 project source/object pairs and 36 direct external objects before building. The producer and independent dependency captures agree, and all hashes remained unchanged through finalization.
- Seven independent Lean regressions passed with clean axiom footprints: pre-gate read obligation despite no write; no writes by pid1; all constexpr mask boundaries; translated byte permission access; nonidentity native-to-backing load; retained allocator contents outside stores; and the exact grid parser boundary.
- Re-executed all 33 original-AST offline compiler profiles: 23 generated and backend-compiled, 10 rejected at the expected frontend/signature stage. All 115 raw/frontend/backend/executable-PTX artifacts match after source-path normalization. PTX debug differences are retained and excluded only after checking no later executable entry/function. No GPU was executed.
- Independently checked 264 frontend configurations across all 22 dtypes and twelve N boundaries. Results: 143 generated, 15 nonrepresentable integers, 22 ordered-comparison failures, 24 unsupported signatures, and 60 absent mappings. Generated raw IR checks confirm signedness/widening at every boundary. Twelve real CPython signed-int parser probes match the modeled grid threshold.
- Re-executed 73 exact-wrapper CPU/launch-stub records. All semantic metadata match; 146 tensor records independently satisfy raw data-pointer affine/null rules. Allocator addresses and uninitialized bytes are retained in evidence but excluded from cross-run equality. Fresh output intervals were checked. These are CPU metadata and launch stubs, not CUDA launches.
- Checked all 404 cases, public statement hash, unchanged schema validators, exact source-stage/dtype/branch links, all 68 numerical ports, and 68 omitted-port mutations. Twenty adapter/queue tamper mutations were rejected, including source/helper/dependency/runtime/IR changes, bad receipt/review pins, producer self-approval, wrong review subject, path escape, missing/changed numerical authority. Whole-queue equality passed in an isolated additive fixture using the actual frozen producer receipt and an explicitly synthetic review. It is not root approval or final repository integration.

There was no GPU execution, native scalar conformance validation or live CUDA permission measurement. Truthful ordinary Torch allocation/metadata and runtime/MMU address-access mapping remain the explicit primitive boundary. Exact numerical conformance is deferred only through the eight listed scalar families. Review work modified only the new /tmp review directory; the frozen candidate, repository source/object dependencies and Git were not modified. All attempted reviewer scripts succeeded on their first execution; all producer failed build attempts remain frozen and indexed by the original candidate receipt.
'''
